// Smoke case (ESP32-C3), second half of `c3-isolation`: the PMP proof from the
// wrong side of the fence.
//
// This child stores a word into the kernel's own memory. If the PMP program
// over the real C3 map is right, the access faults, the child dies and root
// carries on. If the kernel's rows were left open, or U-mode were not
// default-deny, the store would land in the kernel's data and the run would
// come apart elsewhere with no diagnostic naming the cause. So the
// transcript's fault line is the assertion: a broken protection program fails
// by its absence rather than by a wrong value.
//
// Unlike `tests/child-poke`, this child names an address. That case is
// arch-free because both virt profiles put every process region on a grid of
// equal-sized regions, so a child can round its own address down to find its
// base and its neighbour's top. That arithmetic is a fact about the virt map;
// this board has little SRAM and regions sized to fit the kernel, not to tile
// a grid. There is no rounding that finds a neighbour here.
//
// The target is the kernel's memory rather than root's. Root's memory is
// denied because the domain was reloaded at the switch; the kernel's is denied
// because U-mode matches no PMP entry there at all. The second is the property
// the protection model rests on, and the one a board port can get wrong by
// programming entries at the wrong addresses.

// The `sos` module is imported for the runtime, not the API: it pulls `sosrt`
// into this compilation unit, and `sosrt` defines the `__saw_rt_*` seams every
// freestanding image needs. A child holding no handles calls no op through
// it; parking its (absent) System handle makes those seams mute rather than
// absent.
import sos.{System}

/// The first word of the C3's SRAM, which `hal/riscv32-esp32c3/kernel/
/// esp32c3.ld` gives to the kernel's own `.data` and which the kernel grants
/// to nobody. It is where `RAM_BASE` is, so every process region on this
/// board is strictly above it.
static KERNEL_OWNED: UInt = 0x4037_C000

/// A value with no other reason to exist, so a debugger finding it in the
/// kernel's memory would know exactly what put it there.
static POKE_VALUE: UInt = 0xD00D

/// Store into the kernel's memory. Never returns on a correct kernel.
///
/// `unsafe` because it names an `UnsafeMemory`; the unsafety stops here and
/// `_start` stays an ordinary safe signature.
func poke_the_kernel() unsafe {
    UnsafeMemory<UInt, Normal>(KERNEL_OWNED).write(POKE_VALUE)
}

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    // Park the handle the kernel gave this process, which is nothing. The
    // runtime's writer checks it and stays silent, so a panic here costs a
    // fault rather than a garbled line on somebody else's console.
    let _ = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    poke_the_kernel()

    // Unreachable on a correct kernel: the store above faults and the kernel
    // ends this process. A child that got here has been granted the kernel's
    // memory and has no handle to complain with, so it spins and the harness
    // catches the missing fault report.
    while { }
}
